-- chem/fermi.cub — operators of fermions as terms with a normal form, over any ring of coefficients. A letter is a
-- creator or an annihilator of a mode; a word is a product of letters; an operator is a list of (word, coefficient).
-- The rules are the three of exclusion: a_p a†_q = δ_pq − a†_q a_p, two creators (two annihilators) exchange with a
-- sign, and the square of a letter is zero. The normal form has creators first, then annihilators, each by increasing
-- mode; `fa-put` moves one letter into a normal word by those rules, and a product is letters put in one at a time.
-- Two operators are equal exactly when their normal forms agree (`fa-eq`): an operator identity (a certificate that
-- H − λ is a sum of squares B†B is one) is decided by this, with no matrix built and for every number of particles at
-- once. The coefficient ring is an argument (its addition `ad`, multiplication `mu`, the rationals in it `iq`, its
-- zero test `zt`): at the rationals an identity is about numbers, at polynomials (chem/algebra.cub) it is about every
-- value of the symbols, so a statement about an operator given by tensors is written once with the tensors as
-- variables. The adjoint here does not conjugate coefficients: the coefficients are real.
import "algebra.hypercubical"

